WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.890 --> 00:00:02.840
This is how it used to work.

00:00:02.840 --> 00:00:05.570
A real estate agent
would lock in the client,

00:00:05.570 --> 00:00:09.070
they'd stage the place, hold an
open house every single weekend
and hope that the right buyer

00:00:11.610 --> 00:00:13.050
walks through that door.

00:00:13.050 --> 00:00:16.160
10 years ago, you could
probably go look at a house

00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:18.310
and circle back two to three weeks later

00:00:18.310 --> 00:00:20.410
and still have an
opportunity to make a bid.

00:00:20.410 --> 00:00:22.770
The first time I received
a lead through Instagram

00:00:22.770 --> 00:00:24.060
my mind was blown.

00:00:24.060 --> 00:00:25.990
If you're seeing something
in a neighborhood

00:00:25.990 --> 00:00:28.530
that is established you
have two to three days

00:00:28.530 --> 00:00:31.200
after the open house in this
market to make an offer.

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:32.860
Our world has shifted so much.

00:00:32.860 --> 00:00:35.590
Folks are getting
comfortable doing business,

00:00:35.590 --> 00:00:38.780
either through social media
or a hundred percent virtual.

00:00:38.780 --> 00:00:41.840
Social media, like
everything else it touches

00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:45.073
has turned the real estate
business upside down.

00:00:53.690 --> 00:00:56.590
You've heard this trope before,
millennials aren't buying

00:00:56.590 --> 00:00:58.730
and owning as much as
their parents' generation.

00:00:58.730 --> 00:01:01.210
Be it a car, a diamond or a home.

00:01:01.210 --> 00:01:04.170
But the lack of ownership
is not always by choice.

00:01:04.170 --> 00:01:07.980
In 2018, a survey found that
89% of millennial Americans

00:01:07.980 --> 00:01:10.830
want to buy a home, they
just can't afford it.

00:01:10.830 --> 00:01:13.470
As of 2019, millennials of all stripes

00:01:13.470 --> 00:01:17.930
owned only 5% of U.S.
housing compared with 15%

00:01:17.930 --> 00:01:19.033
for gen X-ers.

00:01:20.790 --> 00:01:21.840
Millennials, haven't been able

00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:24.320
to make these big ticket
purchases the same way

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:26.130
as their predecessors did.

00:01:26.130 --> 00:01:28.750
Now, this is down to the financial crisis,

00:01:28.750 --> 00:01:31.870
we have rising student loan debt,

00:01:31.870 --> 00:01:33.470
and a higher cost of living.

00:01:33.470 --> 00:01:35.530
Now, millennials are getting older,

00:01:35.530 --> 00:01:38.460
they're earning more
money and saving more.

00:01:38.460 --> 00:01:41.400
So now is about the time where
a lot of them are going out

00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:42.990
and making those purchases.

00:01:42.990 --> 00:01:44.730
And now having been stuck at home

00:01:44.730 --> 00:01:46.900
for over a year the same generation

00:01:46.900 --> 00:01:48.990
that embraced Ubers and Airbnbs

00:01:48.990 --> 00:01:50.950
wants something to call their own.

00:01:50.950 --> 00:01:52.480
I got married and I have a child

00:01:52.480 --> 00:01:54.640
so looking for more space

00:01:54.640 --> 00:01:57.530
and thought about the conventional
route of young and single

00:01:57.530 --> 00:01:59.560
and live in the city, and
then move out to the suburbs

00:01:59.560 --> 00:02:00.820
when you have a kid and a wife.

00:02:00.820 --> 00:02:02.160
I've seen a whole bunch of my friends

00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:05.120
just move on down to the
West Palm beach, Miami area,

00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:06.200
Austin, Texas.

00:02:06.200 --> 00:02:08.050
So they are certainly
moving out of the city

00:02:08.050 --> 00:02:11.580
but space was top of
mind for them moving out.

00:02:11.580 --> 00:02:15.170
We lived in Williamsburg
for about 16 plus years

00:02:15.170 --> 00:02:19.120
and week two in the
pandemic we just realized

00:02:19.120 --> 00:02:19.970
we needed more.

00:02:19.970 --> 00:02:23.150
So we started looking
for a house right away.

00:02:23.150 --> 00:02:25.170
We now live in Goshen, New York,

00:02:25.170 --> 00:02:27.820
which is about an hour and
a half from New York City.

00:02:28.850 --> 00:02:30.870
Instagram accounts like Circle Houses

00:02:30.870 --> 00:02:32.990
and Cheap Old Houses offer a glimpse

00:02:32.990 --> 00:02:34.770
into the perfect suburban life.

00:02:34.770 --> 00:02:37.160
Often at a price point, not too dissimilar

00:02:37.160 --> 00:02:39.730
to what adults pay for
rent and major cities.

00:02:39.730 --> 00:02:42.530
By the end of 2020 New
Yorkers who didn't flee,

00:02:42.530 --> 00:02:45.890
paid an average of $2,700
a month for an apartment

00:02:45.890 --> 00:02:48.723
even as rent prices plummeted
during the pandemic.

00:02:49.750 --> 00:02:52.700
Millennials make a large
percentage of spending right now

00:02:52.700 --> 00:02:56.030
and that makes them a
valuable consumer for brands.

00:02:56.030 --> 00:02:58.220
That means they're being
courted by everyone out there

00:02:58.220 --> 00:02:59.440
to spend with them.

00:02:59.440 --> 00:03:01.140
They grew up online.

00:03:01.140 --> 00:03:02.950
They're comfortable with
pretty much anything

00:03:02.950 --> 00:03:04.110
that you can do there.

00:03:04.110 --> 00:03:07.020
And that's buying things, selling things,

00:03:07.020 --> 00:03:08.960
Craigslist, eBay, all that.

00:03:08.960 --> 00:03:11.160
Making a hundred dollars
purchase on Craigslist

00:03:11.160 --> 00:03:13.980
or a a thousand dollars purchase on eBay.

00:03:13.980 --> 00:03:17.050
It's not that much of a
leap to go even further

00:03:17.050 --> 00:03:19.280
if you're buying a fancy piece of jewelry

00:03:19.280 --> 00:03:22.210
or a house for a million bucks.

00:03:22.210 --> 00:03:23.690
The uptick in home demand

00:03:23.690 --> 00:03:25.010
fueled by the pandemic

00:03:25.010 --> 00:03:27.140
combined with record low interest rates

00:03:27.140 --> 00:03:29.660
has made home buying extremely competitive

00:03:29.660 --> 00:03:30.890
over the last year.

00:03:30.890 --> 00:03:33.920
So much so that some
millennials look to social media

00:03:33.920 --> 00:03:37.723
to shop and actually buy their
first house sight unseen.

00:03:38.620 --> 00:03:41.950
I saw it sort of something
on my Instagram feed

00:03:41.950 --> 00:03:46.667
and clicked and we ended
up seeing this house.

00:03:46.667 --> 00:03:49.230
The houses weren't really
being shown at that time

00:03:49.230 --> 00:03:50.900
'cause we were in full lockdown.

00:03:50.900 --> 00:03:52.950
We became really interested.

00:03:52.950 --> 00:03:57.673
One YouTube video, 20 Zillow
photos, and that is it.

00:04:02.450 --> 00:04:03.730
The red hot housing market

00:04:03.730 --> 00:04:05.110
spells opportunities

00:04:05.110 --> 00:04:07.450
for developers targeting this demographic.

00:04:07.450 --> 00:04:10.890
Getting eyeballs strictly
through social media.

00:04:10.890 --> 00:04:13.260
People are not looking in the back section

00:04:13.260 --> 00:04:15.730
of the times anymore for
real estate listings.

00:04:15.730 --> 00:04:19.560
Our demographic is between
late twenties to mid forties.

00:04:19.560 --> 00:04:22.700
And that demographic is
spending a lot of time

00:04:22.700 --> 00:04:25.290
on social media and they're
doing a lot of shopping

00:04:25.290 --> 00:04:29.030
via Instagram and other
social media platforms.

00:04:29.030 --> 00:04:31.690
This is Joshua Gelb and Pelle Hamburger.

00:04:31.690 --> 00:04:34.480
During the pandemic, the two
New York city-based developers

00:04:34.480 --> 00:04:37.290
started Nevelhaus, a
new construction project

00:04:37.290 --> 00:04:39.090
two hours north of the city.

00:04:39.090 --> 00:04:41.380
Unlike most traditional
real estate listings,

00:04:41.380 --> 00:04:43.570
the pair bypass the use of a broker

00:04:43.570 --> 00:04:46.270
and advertise their homes
directly on Instagram,

00:04:46.270 --> 00:04:48.140
no fees, no commissions.

00:04:48.140 --> 00:04:50.760
We used to have to hire
a broker, work with them,

00:04:50.760 --> 00:04:52.560
they would do showings on site.

00:04:52.560 --> 00:04:56.320
Now you basically put your
renderings on Instagram

00:04:56.320 --> 00:04:59.620
and within an hour you have 15, 20 replies

00:04:59.620 --> 00:05:01.450
saying "Can I come see
it directly with you?

00:05:01.450 --> 00:05:02.900
And let's see it tomorrow."

00:05:02.900 --> 00:05:05.080
And it's just completely changed the game.

00:05:05.080 --> 00:05:06.530
Most people have that image in their head

00:05:06.530 --> 00:05:11.290
of the quintessential upstate
modern cabin set in the woods.

00:05:11.290 --> 00:05:13.920
We've definitely tried to tap into that

00:05:13.920 --> 00:05:16.600
and create something that's
attainable for these people.

00:05:16.600 --> 00:05:18.156
So you can have that.

00:05:18.156 --> 00:05:20.307
You can be swiping through Instagram

00:05:20.307 --> 00:05:21.807
and that can be your home too.

00:05:22.820 --> 00:05:24.930
There's been quite a proliferation

00:05:24.930 --> 00:05:26.950
of these direct to consumer brands.

00:05:26.950 --> 00:05:28.310
There's just tons of them out there.

00:05:28.310 --> 00:05:30.610
It's not that hard to start up a website

00:05:30.610 --> 00:05:33.810
that sells things and
get things manufactured

00:05:33.810 --> 00:05:35.530
for that E-commerce platform.

00:05:35.530 --> 00:05:40.210
So like Allbirds with shoes,
Casper with mattresses,

00:05:40.210 --> 00:05:42.210
or Warby Parker with glasses,

00:05:42.210 --> 00:05:45.130
all of that means a lot
more opportunity for brands

00:05:45.130 --> 00:05:46.990
in the online shopping space.

00:05:46.990 --> 00:05:49.190
So it's natural that real
estate would wanna get involved

00:05:49.190 --> 00:05:51.503
as well with these big ticket items.

00:05:53.540 --> 00:05:57.040
10 years ago, the broker
would drive the search.

00:05:57.040 --> 00:06:00.260
Now I think that my
clients texts me listings.

00:06:00.260 --> 00:06:01.860
They have alerts.

00:06:01.860 --> 00:06:03.980
They find things quicker
than we do sometimes

00:06:03.980 --> 00:06:05.190
'cause we're out and about.

00:06:05.190 --> 00:06:08.423
I'd say that the buyer is
much more involved these days.

00:06:09.570 --> 00:06:12.930
Social selling is creating
brand new ways to shop.

00:06:12.930 --> 00:06:15.850
So let's look at vintage
clothing for example.

00:06:15.850 --> 00:06:18.670
There are sellers on Instagram
finding successful ways

00:06:18.670 --> 00:06:21.080
to sell one of a kind items.

00:06:21.080 --> 00:06:24.530
It's that fleeting nature,
the ephemeral nature

00:06:24.530 --> 00:06:25.860
of social media.

00:06:25.860 --> 00:06:27.480
You post the item online

00:06:27.480 --> 00:06:30.760
and whoever's first gets
that one of a kind item.

00:06:30.760 --> 00:06:33.820
Now real estate has
similar scarcity to it.

00:06:33.820 --> 00:06:37.703
There's one place to buy,
it's one home, one apartment,

00:06:37.703 --> 00:06:39.140
one thing.

00:06:39.140 --> 00:06:40.520
It's one of a kind.

00:06:40.520 --> 00:06:42.530
But the rise in social media real estate

00:06:42.530 --> 00:06:44.930
isn't just about the
instant gratification.

00:06:44.930 --> 00:06:47.970
Researcher Andre Perry, who
studies economic inclusion

00:06:47.970 --> 00:06:51.120
at the Brookings Institute
says social media has evolved

00:06:51.120 --> 00:06:54.180
from the days of online
hangout spaces and mood boards

00:06:54.180 --> 00:06:56.250
to a powerful change agent.

00:06:56.250 --> 00:07:00.370
Social media is a primary
source to get news

00:07:00.370 --> 00:07:01.320
and information.

00:07:01.320 --> 00:07:03.640
But it's not just about social media,

00:07:03.640 --> 00:07:05.330
it's about the movement that we're in.

00:07:05.330 --> 00:07:08.940
The racial justice
uprising really gave people

00:07:08.940 --> 00:07:11.440
a sense of worth and empowerment.

00:07:11.440 --> 00:07:14.260
And so people not only
are getting information

00:07:14.260 --> 00:07:18.100
about what protects people in the long run

00:07:18.100 --> 00:07:22.220
from economic shocks, things like wealth.

00:07:22.220 --> 00:07:24.680
And so the fastest way
to accumulate wealth

00:07:24.680 --> 00:07:25.830
in this country

00:07:25.830 --> 00:07:28.710
traditionally has been
through home ownership.

00:07:28.710 --> 00:07:31.937
People, millennials in
particular are saying,

00:07:31.937 --> 00:07:35.380
"Hey I want protection.
I wanna live longer.

00:07:35.380 --> 00:07:39.190
I want my education to go further.

00:07:39.190 --> 00:07:41.060
Let me acquire wealth."

00:07:41.060 --> 00:07:45.380
And the fastest way to do that
is through home ownership.

00:07:45.380 --> 00:07:46.380
In his research,

00:07:46.380 --> 00:07:49.130
Andre found that not only are
more millennials buying homes

00:07:49.130 --> 00:07:51.690
during the pandemic, the
rate of home ownership

00:07:51.690 --> 00:07:54.900
among black millennials
are at historic highs.

00:07:54.900 --> 00:07:57.430
When the pandemic hit
many of the amenities

00:07:57.430 --> 00:07:59.427
or the comforts that came along

00:07:59.427 --> 00:08:02.610
with those living conditions were gone.

00:08:02.610 --> 00:08:06.350
Folks couldn't go to bars and fancy shops.

00:08:06.350 --> 00:08:09.910
And so you saw instead of people going out

00:08:09.910 --> 00:08:13.400
and buying clothes, they saved their money

00:08:13.400 --> 00:08:17.230
and bought property and
outside of the city generally.

00:08:17.230 --> 00:08:21.460
People are moving to the
suburbs, to rural areas,

00:08:21.460 --> 00:08:24.610
places where home prices
are a little bit cheaper.

00:08:24.610 --> 00:08:26.340
Many home buyers are looking for houses

00:08:26.340 --> 00:08:28.480
outside the major metropolitan areas.

00:08:28.480 --> 00:08:30.160
Additionally, many home buyers are looking

00:08:30.160 --> 00:08:32.520
for direct outdoor access and added space

00:08:32.520 --> 00:08:34.770
as the uncertainty about
the virus persists.

00:08:34.770 --> 00:08:37.490
Millennials are also
looking to influencers

00:08:37.490 --> 00:08:40.340
not just for the latest
trends and TikTok dances,

00:08:40.340 --> 00:08:42.940
but to partake in important conversations,

00:08:42.940 --> 00:08:45.440
be it about politics or personal finance

00:08:45.440 --> 00:08:46.960
or anything in between.

00:08:46.960 --> 00:08:50.090
Real estate ownership. Let's start small.

00:08:50.090 --> 00:08:51.997
Let's, don't be scared to start small.

00:08:51.997 --> 00:08:55.260
Instead of renting a
big house in the Hills

00:08:55.260 --> 00:08:57.340
and California like most artists do,

00:08:57.340 --> 00:08:59.200
buy you one that's on the flat.

00:08:59.200 --> 00:09:02.120
I meet folks like Brandon Rule

00:09:02.120 --> 00:09:03.970
and Charlamagne tha God.

00:09:03.970 --> 00:09:05.790
I separate them in two forms.

00:09:05.790 --> 00:09:07.000
When you have a residential real estate

00:09:07.000 --> 00:09:08.050
and you have commercial real estate.

00:09:08.050 --> 00:09:10.150
They're talking to you about buying homes.

00:09:10.150 --> 00:09:12.630
When I was coming up really home buying

00:09:12.630 --> 00:09:16.010
with a mysterious shrouded process

00:09:16.010 --> 00:09:19.120
that only a few people could teach me.

00:09:19.120 --> 00:09:23.540
Now I can easily log on
to Clubhouse or Instagram

00:09:23.540 --> 00:09:27.290
and find literally lessons
on how the purchase a home

00:09:27.290 --> 00:09:31.660
but done in with savvy and finesse.

00:09:31.660 --> 00:09:33.550
Today we're gonna make...

00:09:33.550 --> 00:09:34.730
a greenhouse.

00:09:34.730 --> 00:09:36.210
Heather having bought her fixer

00:09:36.210 --> 00:09:39.220
upper sight unseen also look
to internet personalities

00:09:39.220 --> 00:09:41.060
to help figure out how to modernize

00:09:41.060 --> 00:09:44.330
and to make sure her home
was also an investment.

00:09:44.330 --> 00:09:46.310
They gave us a lot of confidence

00:09:46.310 --> 00:09:48.060
and they make it look super easy.

00:09:48.060 --> 00:09:50.780
How we could do all of
those things ourselves.

00:09:50.780 --> 00:09:52.340
My husband just installed a laundry chute

00:09:52.340 --> 00:09:54.080
which is something we
never would have thought of

00:09:54.080 --> 00:09:57.390
if we didn't see an influencer do that

00:09:57.390 --> 00:09:59.623
and how easy it actually is.

00:10:00.970 --> 00:10:02.980
And in the case of
Nevelhaus, Josh and Pelle

00:10:02.980 --> 00:10:04.620
hope that buyers see these homes

00:10:04.620 --> 00:10:06.750
as potential secondary income.

00:10:06.750 --> 00:10:07.653
These young families
look at these products

00:10:07.653 --> 00:10:10.330
or they will look at our homes and say,

00:10:10.330 --> 00:10:11.655
here's a turnkey Airbnb.

00:10:11.655 --> 00:10:13.580
This can serve our family

00:10:13.580 --> 00:10:15.830
but this can also be the perfect rental.

00:10:15.830 --> 00:10:18.780
We've tried to strike a balance
and the design of the home

00:10:18.780 --> 00:10:22.360
for working well as a family
home, that's gonna be lived in

00:10:22.360 --> 00:10:24.840
and also working as an investment.

00:10:24.840 --> 00:10:29.840
A lot of this is points to
how millennials can utilize

00:10:30.245 --> 00:10:33.830
and leverage social
media to their benefit.

00:10:33.830 --> 00:10:37.140
But it also speaks to, again, this moment

00:10:37.140 --> 00:10:39.280
that folks want freedom.

00:10:39.280 --> 00:10:41.530
So millennials are really stepping up

00:10:41.530 --> 00:10:45.340
to this real estate game
and taking ownership

00:10:45.340 --> 00:10:46.433
and building wealth.

00:10:47.520 --> 00:10:48.780
Of course not everything

00:10:48.780 --> 00:10:50.560
is as it seems online.

00:10:50.560 --> 00:10:53.340
While social media can be
a useful tool for learning,

00:10:53.340 --> 00:10:55.180
there's still a lot it can't tell you

00:10:55.180 --> 00:10:58.040
about the home buying experience
or living in the suburbs

00:10:58.040 --> 00:10:59.180
for that matter

00:10:59.180 --> 00:11:03.200
We knew going into it budgeting
for a lot of renovations.

00:11:03.200 --> 00:11:06.670
We kind of thought if we buy it at 450,

00:11:06.670 --> 00:11:09.960
we could put two, 300,000 into the house

00:11:09.960 --> 00:11:12.170
and really make it ours and our own.

00:11:12.170 --> 00:11:16.210
I can't wait to replace
in this kitchen and update

00:11:16.210 --> 00:11:18.250
our builtin deep fryer,

00:11:18.250 --> 00:11:21.950
which not in the listing photos.

00:11:21.950 --> 00:11:23.570
We kind of got into the house

00:11:23.570 --> 00:11:27.850
and we realized that budget
was completely unrealistic.

00:11:27.850 --> 00:11:29.500
That we have a lot of
people who see our design

00:11:29.500 --> 00:11:31.230
on Instagram and they say, "I love it.

00:11:31.230 --> 00:11:34.250
Can you come build this on my property?"

00:11:34.250 --> 00:11:35.580
And then they'll send us the lot

00:11:35.580 --> 00:11:37.940
which they bought sight
unseen during COVID

00:11:37.940 --> 00:11:41.110
and because of site conditions or zoning,

00:11:41.110 --> 00:11:43.160
it's virtually unbuildable.

00:11:43.160 --> 00:11:46.390
I think we all know that
what you see on Instagram

00:11:46.390 --> 00:11:49.020
and what you see in real life
are two very different things.

00:11:49.020 --> 00:11:52.150
So while we can definitely as brokers

00:11:52.150 --> 00:11:54.260
create these fantastic narratives,

00:11:54.260 --> 00:11:57.550
the one recommendation
that I would give is,

00:11:57.550 --> 00:11:58.590
do your homework.

00:11:58.590 --> 00:12:00.080
Don't believe everything you see.

00:12:00.080 --> 00:12:02.130
That's not to say that there's dishonesty

00:12:02.130 --> 00:12:04.490
or anything where people
are trying to manipulate you

00:12:04.490 --> 00:12:07.223
but not everything you see is accurate.

00:12:08.860 --> 00:12:11.550
This shift towards social
media house hunting

00:12:11.550 --> 00:12:13.740
may also help to bring equilibrium

00:12:13.740 --> 00:12:16.560
to a market that has
historically disadvantaged

00:12:16.560 --> 00:12:18.380
specific groups of buyers.

00:12:18.380 --> 00:12:23.380
Many of the traditional ways
that people got mortgages

00:12:23.820 --> 00:12:27.590
or loans often precluded black people

00:12:27.590 --> 00:12:31.640
because they deemed us
too risky for those loans.

00:12:31.640 --> 00:12:35.640
And so with technology, there's
ways to get around that.

00:12:35.640 --> 00:12:40.640
As long as more and more black
people get educated on racism

00:12:41.490 --> 00:12:44.110
and discrimination in lending,

00:12:44.110 --> 00:12:49.110
we're going to figure out ways
to get around that racism.

